Cargo.lock
# fuzz/ is a standalone binary-only crate (never published, never a
# dependency of anything) run only in CI -- committing its lockfile is
# the standard cargo-fuzz convention and is what keeps a loose upstream
# dependency spec (see fuzz/Cargo.toml's ed25519-dalek comment) from
# silently breaking CI again whenever crates.io publishes something new.
!fuzz/Cargo.lock
# Root workspace lockfile (packages/contracts + protocol/) -- these compile
# to deployed WASM artifacts, not a library other Rust projects depend on,
# so the usual "don't commit a library's Cargo.lock" advice doesn't apply
# here; reproducible builds matter more than resolution flexibility. Same
# root cause as fuzz/Cargo.lock: without this, `ethnum` floated to 1.5.2
# and broke CI's contracts build against the current rustc (fixed via
# `cargo update -p ethnum --precise 1.5.3`) with nobody noticing until CI
# finally got far enough to reach that step.
!/Cargo.lock
fuzz/corpus/
fuzz/artifacts/
packages/contracts/test_snapshots/
*.wasm



# ---------------------------------------------
# AI Agent Generated Documentation (Ignored)
# ---------------------------------------------
# Ignoring prompts and plans generated for other networks/hackathons
# Ignore all root markdown files except core documentation
*.md
# 13-sep-2026: the *.md rule above is repo-wide (no leading /), so it was
# also silently swallowing every SKILL.md under .agents/skills/ and
# .claude/skills/ — only nirium-core-context/SKILL.md survived, via a past
# `git add -f`. Everything else (full-context-loading, nirium-github-sweep,
# npm-tarball-verify, cross-repo-content-sync, claude-antigravity-setup)
# existed only on this machine's filesystem, not in git — a fresh clone or
# machine swap would have lost them. First pass only excepted SKILL.md
# itself, then found companion .md files (cross-chain/{cctp,axelar,
# layerzero}.md, pitch-deck/references/*.md, copywriting/references/*.md,
# spec-to-code-compliance/resources/*.md) still silently caught by the same
# rule — widened to the whole directory so no future companion file repeats
# this. Explicit exception, not a `git add -f` workaround, so it survives
# future edits to the *.md list above.
!.agents/skills/**
!.claude/skills/**
!README.md
!DECISION_LOG.md
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!NIRIUM_TECHNICAL_PAPER.md
!DEMO_SCRIPT.md
!SECURITY.md
!SECURITY_AUDIT_V2.md
!claude.md
!AGENTS.md
!IsacapKey.md
!SDKs.md
!MCP_INTEGRATION_GUIDE.md
!MARKETING_PLAN_APRIL_2026.md
!Pitch_Alberto_Stellar_House_CDMX.md
!Playbook_Stellar_House_CDMX.md
!SCF_Pitch_Deck_Completo.md
!Elevator_Pitches.md
# Servable legal docs live under public/ — not internal docs, must ship.
!apps/web/public/legal/*.md
!CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
!LICENSING.md
!SCF_Pitch_Deck_REFINED.md
!INTERNAL_SECURITY_AUDIT.md
!supanirium.md

# ---------------------------------------------
# Legal-sensitive docs: explicit rule, not just *.md
# ---------------------------------------------
# 20-ago-2026: PAQUETE-ABOGADO-2026.md ended up committed (b117f70) with no
# rule covering it at all — the *.md pattern above never protected it, an
# earlier oversight, not a later exception overriding it. Stripped from
# history and given its own explicit rule here so a future edit to the *.md
# exception list above can't silently re-expose it the same way.
PAQUETE-ABOGADO-2026.md

# 20-ago-2026: NiriumMainnet.md is different from the case above — the rule
# for it already existed (see the "Internal product strategy docs" list
# further down), added in the very same commit (1a384b9, 16-jul-2026) that
# ALSO force-added the file to git anyway (git add -f), contradicting the
# rule it had just written. A gitignore rule can't un-track a file once
# tracked — that's what let it stay committed for over a month despite the
# rule existing the whole time. Stripped from history (4 commits rewritten,
# main rebase); no new line needed here, the existing one below already
# covers it correctly now that the file is untracked again.

